Title: A declared phase alphabet and a total setPhase, with transition conditions
Created: 2026-09-16
Author: MinhHaDuong

--- log ---
2026-09-16T00:00Z MinhHaDuong created

--- body ---

## Context

Author, 2026-09-16, after a night in which the same defect surfaced three times
in three disguises: "Je veux les états clairs, et les conditions de transition
aussi."

The framing he gave it is the right one — code proof, critical infrastructure,
Ada and Rust. Judged by that standard `state.phase` fails on the first
criterion: **its alphabet is not declared anywhere, and it is not closed.**
Two assignment sites copy in, verbatim, whatever string `host.blocked()`
returned. The scheduler names seven phases and then stores an open set of
others it has never enumerated.

Three consequences, all observed on 2026-09-15, all the same missing
exhaustiveness check:

- `SDT_PHASE_LABELS` carries fourteen entries, `SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES` five, and
  they are maintained in parallel with nothing linking them. `native-worker-busy`
  sits in the first and not the second — correct, as it happens, since it is a
  wait and not a block, but nothing in the code says so and ticket 0797's body
  filed it wrongly on exactly that reading.
- Ticket 0797's state table has to special-case the sync phase **by literal
  name**, because membership in `SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES` maps to a different row.
- `tests/sdt_sitter_scheduler.mjs` discovers the phase set **by regular
  expression over the source**, with a `phases.size >= 12` floor. Auditing by
  scraping is what a codebase does when it has no declaration to read.

## What this ticket builds

### 1. One declaration, and everything derives from it

A single frozen table — the alphabet — where each phase carries what is today
spread across two tables and several `if` chains: its **axis** (activity,
reason, intent, outcome), its **cadence class**, its **label id** (or the
deliberate `null` that `ready` and `waiting` carry), and whether it is
**terminal**.

`SDT_PHASE_LABELS` and `SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES` stop being hand-maintained lists
and become projections of it. That alone makes the `native-worker-busy` class
of misfiling impossible rather than merely absent.

### 2. A total `setPhase`

Every assignment goes through one function, and that function is **total**: a
value outside the alphabet is not written silently. It is refused, and the
attempt is emitted as a defect record so that "the host said a word we do not
know" becomes an observable event instead of a silent state. This is a range
check at the point of assignment, in the Ada sense, and it is the whole reason
the funnel is worth building — not `phaseSince`, which is a separate matter and
is addressed below under what this ticket does NOT claim.

### 3. The transition conditions, declared

The author asked for these explicitly, and they are the part with the real
design risk. A `from × to` relation, each edge carrying the condition that
licenses it, checked by `setPhase` and enumerable by a test.

The eleven assignment sites today, cited by symbol because the line numbers
moved three times on 2026-09-15 alone — in `scheduler.js`'s `pump()` unless
noted:

| from | to | what licenses it |
|---|---|---|
| `switched-off` | `ready` | the loop re-entered after a `start()` |
| any | `census` | a reconciliation is due |
| `census` | `ready` | the census closed |
| any | `draining` | the dirty set is non-empty at the drain |
| any | `waiting` | no admissible candidate, and (since 0796) no backlog |
| any | *a reason* | `host.blocked()` returned one at candidate gating |
| any | *an admission reason* | `beforeSubmit` refused |
| any | `extracting` | a document was admitted |
| `extracting` | `waiting` | that document settled |
| any | `error` | the sweep threw |
| any | `switched-off` | `disarmSDTSitter` in `bootstrap.js` |

Two of these rows are the whole problem in miniature: *a reason* and *an
admission reason* are not states, they are a diagnosis written into the state
variable, and they come from a vocabulary the scheduler does not own.

## The objection this ticket must answer, because a decorrelated review raised it

A Fable review of the wider re-architecture argued a transition graph is not
writable here: the scheduler would have to learn `bootstrap.js`'s `blocked()`
vocabulary, which is the layering the codebase keeps separate on purpose —
`SDT_STATUS_CLASSES`'s own comment says its class is "deliberately NOT
`blocked`" because `host.blocked()` answers a different question.

The answer this ticket proposes, and which the implementer must defend or
abandon rather than assume: with the alphabet declared in **one place both
sides read**, the scheduler is not learning the host's words — the two are
reading a shared declaration. That is a different arrangement from the one the
review refuted. If, on contact with the code, it turns out the host must be
free to invent reasons at runtime, then the honest outcome is a **partial**
relation: edges declared for the closed part, an explicitly wildcarded edge for
reasons, and the wildcard named as a gap rather than passed off as coverage.

## What this ticket does NOT claim

The same review demolished two payoffs that were originally attached to the
funnel, and they are not resurrected here:

- **`phaseSince` would lie in the case that matters.** The abort path
  deliberately leaves `phase` stale at `extracting`, so an age computed at
  assignment reports the age of a phase the machine has left. And the journal
  already records `phase` with `elapsedMS` every sixty seconds — both incident
  files of 2026-09-15 were reconstructed from exactly that. A `phaseSince`
  field is a second copy of a fact the journal owns.
- **No tick re-derivation.** Half the reason axis is per-candidate —
  `storage-unavailable` and `low-disk` read the candidate's own directory — so
  a tick with no candidate cannot compute it without guessing.

## Relevant files

- `plugins/sdt-sitter/scheduler.js` — the ten assignment sites in `pump()` and
  the sweep's error path; cite by symbol.
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js` — `SDT_PHASE_LABELS`, `SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES`,
  `blocked()`, `nextSweepDelayMS`, `describeSDTSwitchLine`,
  `describeSDTSwitchKind`, `describeSDTTooltip`, `getSDTCoverage` (reads
  `ready`), `toggleSDTSwitch` (reads `switched-off`), `disarmSDTSitter`.
- `tests/sdt_sitter_scheduler.mjs` — **the regex phase discovery and its
  `phases.size >= 12` floor.** A funnel silently empties two of its three
  extractors; the floor is the only thing that would turn that into a failure,
  and it must be re-verified with a positive control before it is trusted.
- `verification/probes/sdt_sitter_scheduler_mutants.py` — mutants anchored on
  the exact assignment text. Establish whether an unmatched anchor fails the
  probe or passes vacuously; if it passes vacuously, that is a finding in its
  own right.
- `tests/test_sdt_sitter.py` — parses the `SDT_PHASE_LABELS` literal as JSON, so
  a table keyed by axis breaks the parser.
- `tests/sdt_sitter_bootstrap.mjs`, `tests/sdt_sitter_dialog.mjs` — fixtures
  that set `phase` directly.

## Two one-way doors

- **The journal schema.** `heartbeat.phase`, `announce phase=` and
  `sweep-end.phase` are read by `bench/sdt_stall_probe.py`, the phase probes,
  and every incident JSON under `verification/incidents/`. A two-field record
  makes past incidents incomparable with future ones. Decide explicitly, and if
  the record changes, say how an old incident is still readable.
- **`Zotero.SDTPackSitter.state`** is the console surface the author's own live
  readings went through. Its shape is an external contract now.

## Test

Red first, and the alphabet makes property tests possible where only example
tests existed:

- For **every** phase in the declared table: its label id resolves, its cadence
  class is defined, and its axis is one of the declared axes. Today this cannot
  be written, because there is no table to iterate.
- `SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES` and `SDT_PHASE_LABELS` each equal their projection of the
  alphabet — which is the assertion that would have caught the
  `native-worker-busy` misfiling.
- `setPhase` refuses a value outside the alphabet and emits the defect record.
  The control: feed it a plausible-looking unknown reason and assert the record,
  not merely that nothing crashed.
- One declared transition and one undeclared one, asserted in both directions.

Every threshold or set boundary introduced gets an assertion on its exact value
and one a unit off, and the mutant is **run**, not assumed.

## Invariants

- No behaviour change in what the sitter does. This is an audit surface, not a
  policy change.
- `ready` and `waiting` stay distinct: `getSDTCoverage` computes `known` from
  `phase !== 'ready'`, and `ready` is deliberately silent to absorb the
  per-document admission gap.
- 0796's drain budget, 0795's phase and 0797's table keep working unchanged.
- No new periodic cost while idle.

## Exit criteria

The set of phases is declared in one place, both derived tables are projections
of it rather than parallel lists, every assignment passes a check that refuses
an unknown value and reports it, and the transitions are declared with their
conditions — completely, or partially with the wildcard named as a gap. A test
iterates the declaration rather than scraping the source for it.
